<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">La Scala</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1778">1778</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Teatro alla Scala</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">La Scala, the Teatro alla Scala, is the principal opera house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. Designed by Giuseppe Piermarini after the old ducal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> burned down, it opened in 1778 under Austrian rule on the site of the church of Santa Maria alla Scala, from which it takes its name. It staged premieres by Rossini, Bellini, Donizetti, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-verdi">Verdi</a> and Puccini and remains one of the world's leading opera houses.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/la-scala.jpg" alt="La Scala" width="320" height="205" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: John Picken · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Exterior_Teatro_Alla_Scala_high_quality_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The previous <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, the Teatro Regio Ducale, was destroyed by fire on 25 February 1776 after a carnival gala. Ninety wealthy Milanese who owned boxes in it asked Archduke Ferdinand of Austria-Este for a new theatre. The neoclassical architect Giuseppe Piermarini produced a first design, which Count Firmian, the governor of Austrian Lombardy, rejected. A second plan was accepted in 1776 by Empress <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a>. The church of Santa Maria della Scala was deconsecrated and demolished to make room, and the new house took its name from it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> was inaugurated on 3 August 1778 as the Nuovo Regio Ducale Teatro alla Scala, with the premiere of Antonio Salieri's Europa riconosciuta. It had six tiers of boxes and above them the loggione, two galleries. The building costs were covered by the sale of boxes, which their owners decorated lavishly, impressing observers such as Stendhal. La Scala soon became the preeminent meeting place of noble and wealthy Milanese. The main floor had no chairs and spectators stood, the orchestra was in full sight because no pit had yet been built, and the foyer served as a casino.</p><p>The house hosted the first productions of operas by Rossini, Bellini, Donizetti, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giuseppe-verdi">Verdi</a> and Puccini, including Norma in 1831, Nabucco in 1842, Otello in 1887 and Turandot in 1926. Verdi for several years refused to let his work be played there after a disagreement over Giovanna d'Arco in 1845, saying the orchestra had corrupted his music, but he conducted his Requiem there on 25 May 1874.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The original structure was renovated in 1907 and given its current layout with 1,987 seats. Bombing severely damaged the house in 1943, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. It was rebuilt and reopened on 11 May 1946 with a concert conducted by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arturo-toscanini">Arturo Toscanini</a> and a soprano solo by Renata Tebaldi. The Filarmonica della Scala orchestra was established in 1982. A major renovation by the architect Mario Botta, which preservationists feared would lose historic details, kept the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> closed from January 2002 to December 2004, when it reopened with Salieri's Europa riconosciuta.</p><p>The season opens each year on 7 December, the feast of Saint Ambrose, patron saint of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. The loggioni, regular occupants of the galleries, are known as the most critical aficionados, giving artists who fail what the tradition calls a baptism of fire.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “La Scala” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the eighteenth century Austrian Lombardy became a centre of the Italian Enlightenment: Pietro Verri’s journal Il Caffè and Cesare Beccaria’s On Crimes and Punishments (1764) influenced penal reform across Europe, and the <b>Teatro alla Scala</b> opened in 1778.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Duchy of Milan · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>In 2019 the tenor Juan Diego Flórez sang it at <b>La Scala</b> in Milan, accompanying himself on the guitar, in a performance watched 17 million times on YouTube.</blockquote><a href="/songs/cucurrucucu-paloma">Cucurrucucú paloma</a></li><li><blockquote>Mussolini stayed away from the premiere of Puccini's Turandot at <b>La Scala</b> on 25 April 1926 because Toscanini would not play it first.</blockquote><a href="/songs/giovinezza">Giovinezza</a></li><li><blockquote>Despite a reported vow to give up opera, he was under contract to <b>La Scala</b>, and its director, Bartolomeo Merelli, pressed the libretto on him.</blockquote><a href="/songs/va-pensiero">Va, pensiero (Chorus of the Hebrew Slaves)</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Teatro alla Scala</b> opened with Salieri's opera L'Europa riconosciuta.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-3">3 August in history</a></li></ul></section>
